'''KOMPSAT-3''' ('''Korean Multi-purpose Satellite-3'''), also known as '''Arirang-3''',<ref name="GSP">{{cite web|url=https://space.skyrocket.de/doc_sdat/kompsat-3.htm|title=KOMPSAT 3 (Arirang 3) |publisher=Gunter's Space Page|date=7 July 2020|access-date=22 October 2021}}</ref> is a South Korean multipurpose [[Earth observation satellite]]. It was launched from [[Tanegashima Space Center]], Japan at 16:39 [[Coordinated Universal Time|UTC]] on 17 May 2012. Like the earlier [[KOMPSAT-1]] and [[KOMPSAT-2]] satellites, it takes its name from the popular Korean folk song [[Arirang]]. Its launch was the culmination of a project begun in 1995.<ref name="ESA1">{{cite web|url=https://directory.eoportal.org/web/eoportal/satellite-missions/k/kompsat-1|title=KOMPSAT-1|publisher=ESA eoPortal Directory|date=2021 |access-date=22 October 2021}}</ref>

KOMPSAT-3 orbits at a height of {{cvt|685.1|km}}, circling the [[Earth]] 14 times per day, and is expected to maintain that orbit for 4 years. It weighs {{cvt|980|kg}}. The satellite carries an Advanced Earth Imaging Sensor System (AEISS), which can distinguish to a 70-cm resolution, allowing the identification of individual vehicles on the ground.<ref name="ESA3"/>

The satellite was succeeded by [[KOMPSAT-5]] and [[KOMPSAT-3A]], which were launched on 2013 and 2015 respectively.

== History ==
South Korea started the KOMPSAT programme in 1995 to nurture its national Earth-imaging industry and supply services for remote-sensing applications. The South Korean KOMPSAT-3 Earth-imaging satellite was developed by [[Korea Aerospace Industries]] (KAI) and [[Korea Aerospace Research Institute]] (KARI), in partnership with [[Astrium|EADS Astrium]], to assure continuity with the KOMPSAT-2 satellite launched in 2006. KOMPSAT-3 was orbited on 17 May 2012 by a launch vehicle from Tanegashima Space Center, Japan. [[Satrec Initiative|SI Imaging Services]] is the worldwide exclusive distributor of KOMPSAT imagery since November 2012.<ref name="ESA3">{{cite web |url=https://directory.eoportal.org/web/eoportal/satellite-missions/k/kompsat-3|title=KOMPSAT-3|publisher=ESA eoPortal Directory|date=2021|access-date=24 October 2021}}</ref>

== Technologies ==
=== Orbit ===
KOMPSAT-3 operates in a near-polar, circular [[Sun-synchronous orbit]]. The orbital parameters are:
* Mean altitude: 685.1&nbsp;km
* Mass: 980&nbsp;kg
* Inclination: 98.13° (Sun-synchronous orbit)
* Orbital period: 98.5 minutes
* Orbital cycle: 28 days

=== Instruments ===
KOMPSAT-3's instruments are designed to acquire high- and very-high-resolution imagery with a footprint of 16.8&nbsp;km. The satellite has the capacity to acquire 20 minutes of imagery on each orbit and it can steer its sensors both ways out to 30° off track. Panchromatic and multispectral images can be acquired at the same time.

